@baseUrl = http://localhost:5191

### 1. Register a new user (expect 201 Created, role = Employee)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "sara",
  "email": "sara@acme.com",
  "password": "Password123"
}

### 2. Register the same user again (expect 409 Conflict)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "sara",
  "email": "sara@acme.com",
  "password": "Password123"
}

### 3. Register with invalid data (expect 400: bad email, short password)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "bad",
  "email": "not-an-email",
  "password": "123"
}

### 4. Login with correct credentials (expect 200 + token)
# @name login
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "sara@acme.com",
  "password": "Password123"
}

### Saves the token from request 4 so the requests below can reuse it
@token = {{login.response.body.token}}

### 5. Login with wrong password (expect 401 Unauthorized)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "sara@acme.com",
  "password": "WrongPassword"
}

### 6. Login with an email that doesn't exist (expect 401, same as wrong password)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "nobody@acme.com",
  "password": "Password123"
}

### 7. Call a protected endpoint WITHOUT a token
# Expect 401 once you add .RequireAuthorization() to the employees group
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/employees

### 8. Call a protected endpoint WITH the token (expect 200)
# Run request 4 first so {{token}} has a value
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/employees
Authorization: Bearer {{token}}

### 9. Call with a fake token (expect 401)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/employees
Authorization: Bearer this.is.not.a.real.token
